ons would fare better in the  future for other patients as well  so ultimately this will be a win-win  situation where more patients are  getting more treatment options  in exchange for us at least from our  business model we are  in exchange of this no charge a no fee  service for patients they are providing  us their anonymized data for you so we  can improve it  improve the system for future patients  uh and this approach will generate more  data  which will be beneficial for both  physicians and the pharmaceutical  companies  uh so they can develop new therapies  faster each turn  through the virtuous firewall will help  improve the lives of future cancer  patients as well  and here's some initial validation data  when we were  initially in development phase of this  algorithm a while ago  so looking at the masala brain cancer  registry  uh in the last two decades of about 451  glioma patients so these are not  randomized self-reported  and based on the data that was collected  via web-based patient reported outcome  survey  we fit a bayesian multi-level model to  this virtual  trial data and we were able to  accurately predict the outcomes of  several large  faiths three these are safety and  efficacy  randomized clinical trials and so with  a little over a third of the patient  numbers that's needed for these  three set of trials so 451  compared to close to 1500  we were able to directionally  project the log survival ratios as you  can see on the right hand side  for these combinations for low mustine  and temozolomide versus  mine alone or a lomastine plus beverages  in mind  versus um lomastine  and also temozolomide plus beverages  versus uh  zolamide alone and so as you can see  here we were able to project that the  log survival ratio  directionally uh is better with the  combination of lomastine  plus tomozilla line  and here's another patient that has went  through our platform where they were  an exceptional responder to um op 201  through our managed access program or  expanded access program  so this was a pediatric patient with  h3k27n mutation  diffuse midline gliomas of the thalamus  this tends to be this is a great for um  cancer so very severe this patient was  wheelchair bound  and have already failed three lines of  therapy  and he received onto a one via our  expanded access program like i mentioned  earlier  and he has an ongoing partial response  to single agent of optional one  which led to an 81 reduction on tumor  load  from the start of antoine which was  incredible  uh the patient right now after two years  continues to do very well  uh on a single agent of onto one he's  now attending school  and can now also ride a bicycle as well  compared to when he was wheelchair bound  two years ago  and so gathering this real world data  for pharmaceutical companies are very  valuable  just to give you an example for this  brain cancer drug  about 20 of the data from  managed access programs was being used  as part of a submission  integrated analysis submission uh with  the clinical trial data  for a new drug application with the fda  um so  so the value of what we are providing  here  is not only to patients but is also  beneficial  for forward companies as well to  accelerate the drug development process  thank you
 

